course: dictionary
lesson: 13390
title: Tugging at the heartstrings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Heartstrings is almost always used in a set phrase: «tug at the heartstrings» or «pull at the heartstrings». The heart is the clue. Heartstrings means your deepest feelings of love and sympathy, so the phrase describes a story or picture that makes you feel these feelings strongly.
explain: Read the words around it. «The sad film tugged at my heartstrings.» A sad film is about people who are hurt or lost, so the film must have made the writer feel sorry and sad. «The lost puppy pulled at everyone's heartstrings» shows the same thing: people felt sorry for it and wanted to help.
explain: Notice the word is plural, even though you only have one heart. Do not worry about why: it is simply the way the phrase is always written. When you see «heartstrings», look for «tug» or «pull» near it, and you will know the phrase means to make someone feel very sorry, sad or loving.
fact: Heartstrings means your deepest feelings of love and sympathy.
fact: «Tug at the heartstrings» means to make someone feel very sorry, sad or loving.
fact: The usual phrases are «tug at the heartstrings» and «pull at the heartstrings».
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole smells his old home, and the call of it tugs at his heartstrings until he weeps.
fact: A sad film, a lost puppy or a charity advert can all tug at the heartstrings.
exercise: «The sad ending of the film tugged at my heartstrings.» What does this mean? | It made my hair tangle in the wind | It made me want to go to sleep | *It made me feel sad or sorry | It made me laugh out loud
exercise: Mia says heartstrings is a real string inside your chest. What does heartstrings mean? | The part of your heart that pumps blood | *Your deepest feelings of love and sympathy | A musical instrument from a band | A string used to tie up parcels
exercise: «The charity advert ___ at my heartstrings, so I gave some money.» Choose the right word for the gap. | counted | *tugged | jumped | painted
test: «The old song pulled at her heartstrings.» What does this suggest about her? | She could not hear the song at all | She was cross with the singer | She wanted to sing along very loudly | *She felt sad, loving or sorry
test: Which phrase is the usual one used with heartstrings? | sing the heartstrings | bake the heartstrings | knit the heartstrings | *tug at the heartstrings
exam: In The Wind in the Willows, what tugs at Mole's heartstrings? | A loud storm on the river | A cold, wet day by the road | A new friend's song in the woods | *The smell of his old home, which calls him back
